# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: release-1.9.0 — cut 1.9.0 before starting cadtui
#+STATUS: TODO
#+PRIORITY: P2
#+TYPE: infra
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: feat-release-1.9.0

* Context

pjb, 2026-08-15: "Avant de commencer cadtui, il faudra bumper la version a
1.9.0 et faire une release."

So 1.9.0 is a boundary, not a routine bump: the 1.8 series closes on the
work done to date, and =cadtui= — an 882-line specification for a new host
that also generalises =cador= — opens on a fresh series. Cutting first means
the series that shipped the codec, the interactor configuration, the dialect
warning register and this week's Windows/CAD fixes is frozen and installable
before a large refactor starts moving =cador= underneath it.

=m= (not =d=) is the right digit: the 1.8 series added user-visible features
(host-independent single-octet codecs, =lisp.conf=, the =[tag]= diagnostic
format and its manual annex, self-documenting configuration files).

* NOT YET — this is a gate, not a next action

pjb, same day: "Mais ne bump pas la version tout de suite, on a peut-être
d'autres issues à implémenter avant."

So nothing here is to be done on sight. 1.9.0 is the gate that must be
crossed *before cadtui starts*, and whatever else ships first ships INTO
1.9.0. Concretely: keep bumping clautolisp's DEVELOP as usual (1.8.37,
1.8.38, …) while more work lands; the stamps below are set once, in the
release commit itself, not incrementally.

Cutting early would be the actual mistake — it would either strand the
issues finished afterwards in a 1.9.1 nobody planned, or tempt a
re-tag.

* What the version numbers must become

Measured against =release-1.8.0=, not assumed:

| program        | now    | must become | why |
|----------------+--------+-------------+-----|
| clautolisp     | 1.8.36 | *1.9.0*     | changed throughout the series |
| alfe           | 1.8.3  | *1.9.0*     | 11 commits since release-1.8.0 |
| read-autolisp  | 1.6.0  | *unchanged* | 0 commits since release-1.8.0 |

The rule is =version-rules.md= "a program changed within a series must have
A.B = M.m; a program that has not changed may stay at an older A.B and still
ship". read-autolisp staying at 1.6.0 in a 1.9.0 release is therefore
correct, not an oversight — worth writing down, because it looks wrong.

Check with =bash scripts/manifest-versions.sh= (prints the three stamps) and
=git log release-1.8.0..master -- <path>= per program.

* Procedure (AGENTS.md § Cutting a release; full text in version-rules.md §5)

1. bump the shipped programs' stamps, update =RELEASE_NOTES.org=, commit;
2. =git tag -a release-1.9.0=;
3. fast-forward =version-1.9= and =version-1= onto that commit — =version-1.9=
   must be CREATED, since d = 0;
4. push the tag and both pointers to =origin=;
5. post-release bump on master, so no untagged commit claims a released
   version;
6. =glab release create release-1.9.0=.

* Before tagging

- =make check-versions= must pass (it does today: 14 releases, 10 series, all
  invariants hold — run =git fetch --prune --tags= first);
- =RELEASE_NOTES.org= already carries this series' entries; give 1.9.0 a
  heading rather than leaving them under the running section;
- the CI lane parity rule applies (=clautolisp-ci-github-gitlab-parity=):
  mirror the common Linux lanes into =.github/workflows/ci.yml= before a
  release tag, by hand;
- on a =release-*= tag the whole pipeline runs automatically and the
  release-mandatory jobs BLOCK it (see the =.gate-*-hard= templates), so the
  native runners should be up when the tag is pushed — including the CAD
  machine, which is a laptop. Budget it: a full CAD sweep is ~30 minutes of
  exclusive runner time (=documentation/ci-job-durations.md=).

* Note

Create no =clautolisp-v*= / =alfe-*= tag: those formats are deprecated, a
program version is read from its source.
